1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,336
Welcome to lectures twenty three and twenty four:

2
00:00:03,336 --> 00:00:12,120
data transformation and feature scaling, using one real dataset, California Housing, with twenty thousand six hundred forty rows.

3
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:14,322
Three ideas today.

4
00:00:14,322 --> 00:00:15,412
Why transform:

5
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:20,397
skew, heavy tails and mixed units hide patterns and hurt models.

6
00:00:20,397 --> 00:00:21,721
Change the shape:

7
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:23,746
log transform and Box-Cox.

8
00:00:23,746 --> 00:00:25,381
And change the units:

9
00:00:25,381 --> 00:00:28,808
standardisation, min-max and robust scaling.

10
00:00:28,808 --> 00:00:39,478
In the lab we apply every method to all seven numeric features and compare the distribution before and after, with statistics and graphs.

11
00:00:39,478 --> 00:00:40,491
The key idea:

12
00:00:40,491 --> 00:00:45,008
transforming changes the shape, scaling changes the units.

13
00:00:45,808 --> 00:00:49,254
California Housing from the nineteen ninety census:

14
00:00:49,254 --> 00:01:01,888
twenty thousand six hundred forty block groups, seven numeric features we transform, two hundred seven missing values in total bedrooms, and a skew of four point nine four for population.

15
00:01:01,888 --> 00:01:08,914
We do not transform longitude and latitude, since they are locations, or ocean proximity, which is text.

16
00:01:08,914 --> 00:01:15,400
And median house value is top-coded at five hundred thousand and one, a real data quality quirk.

17
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:17,155
Four reasons.

18
00:01:17,155 --> 00:01:18,257
Squashed plots:

19
00:01:18,257 --> 00:01:23,621
most points crowd into one corner while the long tail stretches the axis.

20
00:01:23,621 --> 00:01:24,944
Model assumptions:

21
00:01:24,944 --> 00:01:33,174
linear and logistic regression, L D A and many tests work best with symmetric features and linear relationships.

22
00:01:33,174 --> 00:01:34,276
False outliers:

23
00:01:34,276 --> 00:01:38,611
in a long tail, many outliers are just normal large values.

24
00:01:38,611 --> 00:01:39,787
And mixed units:

25
00:01:39,787 --> 00:01:47,061
years, counts and dollars in one model, where the biggest numbers dominate distances and gradients.

26
00:01:47,061 --> 00:01:51,984
The first three call for a transform; the fourth calls for scaling.

27
00:01:52,784 --> 00:01:56,285
Five of seven features are strongly right-skewed.

28
00:01:56,285 --> 00:01:56,999
The guide:

29
00:01:56,999 --> 00:02:05,430
absolute skew below zero point five is roughly symmetric, zero point five to one is moderate, and above one is strong.

30
00:02:05,430 --> 00:02:15,004
Population is four point nine four, total rooms four point one five, bedrooms three point four eight, households three point four one:

31
00:02:15,004 --> 00:02:17,361
count data with long right tails.

32
00:02:17,361 --> 00:02:23,720
Housing median age, at zero point oh six, is already symmetric, so leave its shape alone.

33
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:27,075
Transforming changes the shape.

34
00:02:27,075 --> 00:02:37,790
It is non-linear, so big values are pulled in more than small ones; skewness and kurtosis change, but the order of values is kept.

35
00:02:37,790 --> 00:02:38,532
Examples:

36
00:02:38,532 --> 00:02:41,746
log, square root, Box-Cox, Yeo-Johnson.

37
00:02:41,746 --> 00:02:43,889
Scaling changes the units.

38
00:02:43,889 --> 00:02:44,960
It is linear:

39
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:48,340
subtract a centre and divide by a spread.

40
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:55,263
Skewness and kurtosis stay exactly the same, and features become comparable in size.

41
00:02:55,263 --> 00:02:56,005
Examples:

42
00:02:56,005 --> 00:02:58,395
standard, min-max and robust.

43
00:02:58,395 --> 00:03:02,104
For skewed data, transform first, then scale.

44
00:03:02,904 --> 00:03:06,322
The log transform is y equals log of one plus x.

45
00:03:06,322 --> 00:03:14,795
It compresses large values much more than small ones, and the plus one keeps zero safe, though x must be at least zero.

46
00:03:14,795 --> 00:03:18,427
It turns multiplicative effects into additive ones.

47
00:03:18,427 --> 00:03:22,699
A twelve thousand times range in x becomes about nine units.

48
00:03:22,699 --> 00:03:26,757
It has a fixed strength, the same pull for every feature.

49
00:03:26,757 --> 00:03:28,466
A caution from our data:

50
00:03:28,466 --> 00:03:43,704
log over-corrects the count features, with population skew going from four point nine four to minus one point oh four, and it damages the symmetric housing median age, creating five hundred fifty nine new outliers.

51
00:03:44,504 --> 00:03:47,931
Box-Cox lets the data choose the power lambda.

52
00:03:47,931 --> 00:03:54,040
Y equals x to the lambda minus one, over lambda, and log of x when lambda is zero.

53
00:03:54,040 --> 00:04:00,671
Lambda is chosen by maximum likelihood to make the result as close to normal as possible.

54
00:04:00,671 --> 00:04:05,961
It requires every x above zero; for zeros or negatives use Yeo-Johnson.

55
00:04:05,961 --> 00:04:14,827
The power ladder runs from lambda one, no change, through point five, square root, zero, log, to minus one, reciprocal.

56
00:04:14,827 --> 00:04:15,721
Our lambdas:

57
00:04:15,721 --> 00:04:28,088
housing median age about point eight, almost untouched; the counts around point two two, between square root and log; and median income about point oh nine, near log.

58
00:04:28,888 --> 00:04:30,415
Three ways to scale.

59
00:04:30,415 --> 00:04:31,637
Standard scaler:

60
00:04:31,637 --> 00:04:42,100
z equals x minus mean over standard deviation, giving mean zero and standard deviation one, but outliers stay far away and pull the mean.

61
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:43,246
Min-max scaler:

62
00:04:43,246 --> 00:04:53,251
x minus min over max minus min, putting every value between zero and one, but one extreme value sets the max and squeezes the rest.

63
00:04:53,251 --> 00:04:54,320
Robust scaler:

64
00:04:54,320 --> 00:05:01,881
x minus median over I Q R, giving median zero and I Q R one, ignoring extremes, best with outliers.

65
00:05:01,881 --> 00:05:03,485
All three are linear:

66
00:05:03,485 --> 00:05:08,296
the histogram keeps its shape and only the axis numbers change.

67
00:05:09,096 --> 00:05:11,287
How do we measure the impact?

68
00:05:11,287 --> 00:05:11,967
Symmetry:

69
00:05:11,967 --> 00:05:14,461
skewness should move toward zero.

70
00:05:14,461 --> 00:05:14,914
Tails:

71
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:17,786
large positive kurtosis should shrink.

72
00:05:17,786 --> 00:05:18,466
Outliers:

73
00:05:18,466 --> 00:05:23,226
the I Q R count of tail values should fall inside the whiskers.

74
00:05:23,226 --> 00:05:23,680
Shape:

75
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:28,214
a histogram or density, where one tall spike becomes a bell.

76
00:05:28,214 --> 00:05:29,045
And spread:

77
00:05:29,045 --> 00:05:31,841
box plots comparable across features.

78
00:05:31,841 --> 00:05:41,664
Always compare the number and the picture together, because a statistic can hide a cap, a gap or two peaks that the graph reveals.

